@@ -247,7 +247,7 @@ The record **types** of the multipart commit protocol (proposal 0016, still a dr
 
 `UploadPart`'s staging half gives `slot:` and `sidx:` their first writer: `wyrd_core::multipart::stage::stage_part` (#932). It reserves one in-flight slot, a free index of the session's fixed slot range, under `require_absent` on that key with the session required `Open` on its exact bytes, so the cap is the key space and a slot a crashed or refused attempt leaves counts toward it until the session is reclaimed. It then streams the body into chunks and registers each chunk's owned `sidx:` entry, with the placement its fragments will go to, before any of those fragments is written. That intent batch requires the session `Open` on the bytes the attempt was fenced on, and the absence of a drain record for every server it names (`desired:dserver:<id>`, a key that now lives in core and that the custodian shares), so it fails once the session has left `Open` or a selected server's drain is recorded; a chunk is planned around draining servers and re-planned when a drain lands first, never refused for it. Each intent checks every lease of the attempt at its own clock reading before it is sent, and refuses a lapsed one rather than reviving it. For as long as the request runs, including while a body read or a fragment write is pending (on a timer the caller supplies), one batch moves the slot's lease and every owned lease forward together, half a lease TTL after the last such batch (#933). It rewrites the slot in the same batch as every owned lease it renews, under the session fence, so a reaper fence that pinned the slot bytes it judged cannot land over a request that has since renewed. That renewal never holds staging past a pending write's deadline or the body-idle bound: work that answers while it commits is taken, and the renewal is then awaited to its end; one still committing when such a deadline passes is reported as an unknown outcome. Every round of a renewal checks every lease at its own clock reading, so a lapsed lease is refused, never revived. A renewal that fails, or finds the session gone from `Open`, stops staging at once; if a fragment write is then still pending, or the chunk store could not verify one, the error that stops staging is classed indeterminate, carries the renewal's cause and any doubt about its own commit, and is never a plain refusal. No renewal batch can exceed the per-batch operation budget `B_ops`: a knob set whose largest part would need one fails the knob clamps, and staging refuses a request whose chunk bound would. The staged attempt gives the part commit one last renewal of the same kind, which succeeds only when its leases still cover a span the caller names, judged at a clock reading taken after its last commit returned. Every fragment write carries an authorization deadline measured from the clock reading taken before its chunk's intent was committed, and staging waits for the writes no longer than that deadline: a write still pending then, or one the chunk store could not verify, is counted neither written nor not written, the error that stops staging is classed indeterminate in either case, and whatever such a write leaves sits at a position the chunk's owned entry already names. Every body read is bounded too: a read the client leaves without a non-empty piece for the caller's idle bound stops staging, and a piece or end of body that staging only sees once the bound has passed counts as none. An outcome the store could not report is settled by re-reading the batch's own record, never guessed, and an ambiguous reserve retries only the index it may already hold, so no attempt ever holds two slots. Staging writes no `part:` or `psum:` record and releases nothing: it hands its attempt, with one complete chunk reference per chunk, to the part commit (#927), and nothing calls it in production until the S3 `UploadPart` verb is wired (#669).
 
-The part commit, `wyrd_core::multipart::commit::commit_part` (#1027), gives `part:` and `psum:` their writer: after one last renewal, and only with the dispatch margin left on the lease, one batch lands them with the slot release and the attempt's `sidx:` deletes, and a re-upload puts the replaced part's chunks in a `retire:bytes:` obligation in that same batch. A part past `MAX_PART_CHUNKS` or the cumulative `MAX_STAGED_CHUNKS`, and a settled stop before any commit is sent, compensate in one batch: a `retire:bytes:` obligation for the attempt's own chunks, the slot release and the `sidx:` deletes. `commit::upload_part` composes reserve, stage and commit. A commit answered `Conflict` is classified from a re-read of the part key, the session, its own slot and the session again (#1028): a commit that lost to a live session (a same-number rival, a repair of the prior part, or a Complete that fenced and rolled the session back to a newer `Open` epoch) compensates under that session and is refused `OperationAborted`, since no reaper walks an `Open` session; one that lost to a fence is refused `NoSuchUpload` and leaves its residue to the reaper; a missing session counts as a terminal delete only when a slot read made after it finds the slot key absent, and any record there is damage. A conflict no read explains sends the same batch again, within a bound, and then compensates, but a re-upload first reads the obligation key its commit required absent, and a record there is a stop with no compensation; a damaged record is a stop, never a rival or a fence. A compensation answered `Conflict` is classified the same way and chases a further rollback to its newer epoch, within its own bound. Each commit round is sent only with the dispatch margin left on the lease. One with an unknown outcome still stops in doubt with no further batch; settling it is still to land. Nothing calls it in production until the S3 `UploadPart` verb is wired (#669).
+The part commit, `wyrd_core::multipart::commit::commit_part` (#1027), gives `part:` and `psum:` their writer: after one last renewal, and only with the dispatch margin left on the lease, one batch lands them with the slot release and the attempt's `sidx:` deletes, and a re-upload puts the replaced part's chunks in a `retire:bytes:` obligation in that same batch. A part past `MAX_PART_CHUNKS` or the cumulative `MAX_STAGED_CHUNKS`, and a settled stop before any commit is sent, compensate in one batch: a `retire:bytes:` obligation for the attempt's own chunks, the slot release and the `sidx:` deletes. `commit::upload_part` composes reserve, stage and commit. A commit answered `Conflict` is classified from a re-read of the part key, the session, its own slot and the session again (#1028): a commit that lost to a live session (a same-number rival, a repair of the prior part, or a Complete that fenced and rolled the session back to a newer `Open` epoch) compensates under that session and is refused `OperationAborted`, since no reaper walks an `Open` session; one that lost to a fence is refused `NoSuchUpload` and leaves its residue to the reaper; a missing session counts as a terminal delete only when a slot read made after it finds the slot key absent, and any record there is damage. A conflict no read explains sends the same batch again, within a bound, and then compensates, but a re-upload first reads the obligation key its commit required absent, and a record there is a stop with no compensation; a damaged record is a stop, never a rival or a fence. A compensation answered `Conflict` is classified the same way and chases a further rollback to its newer epoch, within its own bound. Each commit round is sent only with the dispatch margin left on the lease. A commit whose outcome the store could not report is the last batch the attempt sends (#1029): no retry and no compensation, since after a commit lands a late staging batch of the same attempt can put its slot back at the very bytes the commit required, and a same-number rival can write an empty prior's bytes again, so no read can tell a commit that did not land from one that landed and was then rebuilt. Reads alone settle it, in at most two laps of the same four reads: it is answered committed only when its slot is found gone while a session read made after that slot read still finds the exact `Open` bytes the commit required, which only its own commit can do, since a part record names no attempt and an empty part's bytes match another attempt's. Anything else, a slot still held, a session that moved or was deleted, damage or a failed read, ends the request in an error that classifies as indeterminate, says whether the commit may still land and names what ended the reads; its slot stays held until the session ends. A lapsed lease is never taken as evidence either way. A compensation with an unknown outcome still stops in doubt with no further batch; settling it is still to land. Nothing calls it in production until the S3 `UploadPart` verb is wired (#669).
 
 `CompleteMultipartUpload`'s fence phase writes `mpu:` too: `wyrd_core::multipart::complete::fence_complete` (#1007). It answers every state but `Open` from the decision-3 table and writes nothing: another publisher's `Completing` fence is refused `OperationAborted` and left untouched, and a `Completed` tombstone answers only an identical part list, with the publication it recorded. A request for another target than the session's own answers `NoSuchUpload`, and one whose clock source is not the session's is stopped before anything is written, so every stamp in a session comes from one clock. On `Open@E` it first checks the named list: part numbers strictly ascending, every part present in the session's `psum:` range with the named digest, and the bucket marker present. A list that is already invalid is answered without a fence, and a session that has spent `MAX_COMPLETE_ATTEMPTS` fences is refused with nothing written. Otherwise one batch, conditioned on the exact bytes read, moves the session to `Completing@E+1` with the fence time, no segment written, a publish target of the session's own parent, name and new epoch, and one more attempt spent. Behind the fence the list is checked again, against the part set the fence froze, and that check is the one that decides: a part replaced between the two checks, or a bucket deleted, is a rejection. A rejected request releases its fence in one batch conditioned on the fence's own bytes, back to `Open@E+2` with the attempt given back, and then answers the rejection; so a client's mistake never spends the budget, and an `Open` session can always be fenced again. A valid request hands its caller the held fence (the frozen part summaries, the fence epoch and the bucket marker bytes read) and publishes nothing itself: the verb below does. A lost commit is judged from what the store holds, within a bounded number of rounds for the fence and for the release. A commit whose outcome the store could not report ends the request at once in an error that names the upload and classifies as indeterminate. A release that runs out of rounds or that the store refuses for good ends in an error that says the fence still stands, which the reaper's rollback (#625) then clears; neither is ever an answer. A store fault while checking behind the fence is never answered as a rejection, but it still tries one release, so a fault of the store's does not cost the client an attempt or a wait: the error keeps the fault and says the fence was taken down, or that it still stands if that release did not land. If that release finds another writer moved the session to a state with an answer of its own (an abort, a rival's fence, a tombstone, a removed record), the request gets that answer instead, and the fault is not reported. A release that conflicts and whose re-read fails says the fence's state is unknown, since nothing can tell. It has no production caller until the S3 verb is wired (#670).
 
